We have method add(Edge edge). Implementation:

```
public void add(Edge edge) {
    if (nodePath.isEmpty()) {
        add(null, edge);
    } else {
        add(nodePath.peek(), edge);
    }
    // @@@ If the edge is null, then the last element in the path is null; if the edge is not null, then the last element in the path is the added edge.
}
```

We need to refer to observer methods: we have peekEdge() returns the edge at top of stack; also maybe getEdgePath() etc. The comment: "If the edge is null, then the last element in the path is null; if the edge is not null, then the last element in the path is the added edge."

Thus assert should express relationship between input parameter edge and the last element after operation.

We need to use this.peekEdge() presumably to get last element. So assert: (edge == null) => this.peekEdge() == null && (edge != null) => this.peekEdge() == edge.

But need to use equality for objects; in Java, use == for reference equality. In specifications, we can use ==.

Thus final assert statement:

`assert (edge == null) => this.peekEdge() == null && (edge != null) => this.peekEdge() == edge;`

But ensure parentheses for precedence: `=>` lower priority than &&, so need parentheses: `(edge == null) => this.peekEdge() == null && (edge != null) => this.peekEdge() == edge` is parsed as `(edge == null) => (this.peekEdge() == null && (edge != null) => this.peekEdge() == edge)`. That's wrong. Need parentheses around each implication maybe combine with &&: `((edge == null) => this.peekEdge() == null) && ((edge != null) => this.peekEdge() == edge)`. That is safe.

Thus output:

```java
assert ((edge == null) => this.peekEdge() == null) && ((edge != null) => this.peekEdge() == edge);
```

